course: dictionary
lesson: 220
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word alight has more than one meaning, so the words around it tell you which one to use. «Set alight» is about fire: if something is set alight, it has caught fire and is burning.
explain: Alight can also mean shining brightly. When eyes are alight with joy, they shine with happiness. In a story, a face or eyes alight means the character is excited or delighted.
explain: Alight can also be a formal verb meaning to get off a bus or train, or for a bird or insect to land on something. On a bus, «Please alight here» means please get off here.
fact: Alight has several meanings, so the sentence tells you which one to use.
fact: «Set alight» means caught fire. «Could set the gas alight» means a spark could start a fire in the gas.
fact: Eyes or a face can be alight, meaning shining brightly with joy or excitement.
fact: On buses and trains, to alight means to get off. «Alight at the next stop» means get off at the next stop.
fact: A good check is to swap in a simple meaning, such as «catch fire» or «get off», and see if it fits the sentence.
exercise: «If you smell gas, do not switch anything on, because a tiny spark could set the gas alight.» What does alight mean here? | *On fire | Landing on a branch | Getting off a bus | Shining with happiness
exercise: «Her eyes were alight with joy when she opened the present.» What does alight mean here? | Getting off a train | Landing softly on a chair | On fire, with flames | *Shining brightly, full of happiness
exercise: The bus driver says, «Please alight at the next stop.» What does alight mean? | To shine with happiness | To land on a leaf | To catch fire | *To get off
test: «Heaters can set fabric alight, so keep clothes well away from them.» What does alight mean here? | Landing on something | Shining brightly | Getting off a vehicle | *Caught fire and burning
test: «A bird may alight on the fence for a moment.» What does alight mean here? | Set on fire | Get off a bus | Shine with joy | *Land on something
exam: Which meaning of alight fits the bus driver's words, «Alight here, please»? | Landing on a roof | On fire | *Get off the bus | Shining brightly
